  "body": "## What changed\n\n- harden the VCW PNG decoder with fixed-profile dimension checks, CRC validation, bounded compressed and decompressed data, strict chunk ordering, and malformed/trailing-data refusal\n- validate layer-stream lengths, JSON object shape, zero padding, embedded band/index/encoding identity, and cube metadata topology before allocation\n- bound ZIP member expansion and reject duplicate required members\n- make cube publication crash-durable with collision-free same-directory stages, staged/final file fsync, atomic replacement, parent-directory fsync, and reliable stage cleanup\n- mirror the normative standalone codec changes into the Hermes vendor snapshot and refresh the repository-alignment receipt\n- strengthen the existing B-SC invariant and standalone selftest with hostile CRC, dimensions, truncation, decompression, layer-length, swapped-layer, and overlapping-atlas cases\n\n## Why\n\nPortable VCW cubes and spores can cross trust boundaries. The previous decoder trusted attacker-controlled PNG dimensions, lengths, and decompressed output, while `Cube.save` verified a shared `<path>.stage` file but did not durably sync the replacement. This left resource-exhaustion, malformed-topology, stage-collision, and crash-consistency gaps.\n\n## Impact\n\nThe on-disk format remains `vcw-cube-png-v2`. Valid Mantle-produced cubes remain interoperable. Malformed, non-profile, duplicate-member, or topology-inconsistent containers now fail closed.\n\n## Validation\n\n- `python -m mantle check`: 14 passed, 0 skipped, 0 failed\n- `python -m mantle prove`: 90/90 invariants green\n- standalone VCW selftest and engine interoperability: PASS\n- Hermes addon: 135/135 tests PASS against the pinned Hermes commit\n- adversarial containment: 11/11 PASS\n- vendor parity and repository alignment: PASS\n",
